<%# Archived stocks a student still holds.

    **A student who holds an archived stock must be able to sell it.** That is the only action the data
    supports on a closed company, and it is the whole of this section.

    What went: a `<details>` listing every archived stock inside `Stock::LIST_RETENTION`, for any reader.
    It was defensible - it had a date, a window and an exemption - without ever answering who opened it
    and to do what. A student cannot buy any of them; a teacher who wants the catalogue has
    `/admin/stocks`, which carries more. A list nobody can act on is not oversight, it is a list.

    Nothing deletes these rows and nothing should: `orders.stock_id` and `portfolio_stocks.stock_id` are
    both NOT NULL with foreign keys and `dependent: :restrict_with_error`, so a stock a student has ever
    traded cannot be removed without destroying that student's trade history. `Stock::LIST_RETENTION`
    still governs the admin list; what changed is only who is shown one they cannot use. %>
<% held = stocks.select { |stock| current_user.holding?(stock) } %>

<% if held.any? %>
  <%= render "stocks/stocks_table",
             title: "Archived stocks you hold",
             description: "You still own shares in these. They cannot be bought any more, " \
                          "but you can sell whenever you like.",
             stocks: held %>
<% end %>
